#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_range

#% text
# `function_range(f, x, y)` gives the values of `f` over the reals as a condition on `y`; `function_range([f, cond], x, y)` on the part where `cond` holds.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The range is found from the global minimum and maximum (`minimize`, `maximize`), so the function is taken to be continuous on a connected domain.

#% item
# A side without a bound drops out of the condition; `true` means every real value.

#% item
# When an extremum is a limit that is not attained the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A parabola:

#% cell
function_range(x^2, x, y)
#% output
#= y >= 0

#% text
# A cubic takes every value:

#% cell
function_range(x^3, x, y)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# On an interval:

#% cell
function_range([x^2 + 1, -1 <= x and x <= 2], x, y)
#% output
#= 1 <= y and y <= 5

#% text
# A downward parabola:

#% cell
function_range(4 - x^2, x, y)
#% output
#= y <= 4

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The ends are the minimum and the maximum:

#% cell
minimize([x^2 + 1, -1 <= x and x <= 2], x)
#% output
#= [1, [x -> 0]]

#% cell
maximize([x^2 + 1, -1 <= x and x <= 2], x)
#% output
#= [5, [x -> 2]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `minimize`, `maximize`, `function_domain`
